<interrogationes unitas="dispensator">

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I wire an app's event loop through the dispensator?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_creare dispensator_tractare dispensator_pulsare dispensator_arbor dispensator_finire" via="lib/ludus_fenestra.c">Create
      it once with dispensator_creare(piscina, intern, repo, actiones,
      componere, ctx, quies_ms) - NIHIL if any of the first five is
      NIHIL - which composes frame 0 at once. Feed every input event to
      dispensator_tractare, call dispensator_pulsare(d, now) once per
      frame, paint dispensator_arbor(d), and call dispensator_finire
      after the loop ends. ludus_fenestra.c is the window glue that does
      exactly this.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I keep the Componens* from dispensator_arbor across events?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_arbor dispensator_recomponere dispensator_numerus_compositionum">No.
      Trees live in two ping-pong piscinae and each recomposition
      empties the older one; every handled event or pulse recomposes at
      least once (twice when derived events were delivered), so a node
      pointer is good only until the next dispensator_tractare,
      dispensator_pulsare or dispensator_recomponere. Keep the id
      instead: component ids are interned and outlive the tree.
      dispensator_numerus_compositionum counts compositions.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what is the staleness rule, and how often does my componere function run?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_tractare dispensator_recomponere Componere" via="lib/dispensator.worklog.md">After
      every dispatched event, including pulses and events no action
      consumed, the tree is recomposed from the island and Motus, and
      again after any derived events are delivered. Painting is
      separate and happens once per frame outside this library. A
      Componere must only read: it builds the tree into the piscina it
      is given and writes nothing to the island.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I set, push and pop keyboard focus from code?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_focus dispensator_focus_ponere dispensator_focus_impellere dispensator_focus_extrahere insula_attributum_tollere">dispensator_focus_ponere
      writes a component id; dispensator_focus_impellere pushes the
      current focus and sets a new one; dispensator_focus_extrahere pops
      it back. They are the attributes focus and focus_acervus (a
      comma-separated list) in the innermost branch of the focus chain,
      so an island restore restores them. Setting an empty id removes
      the attribute, since the interner refuses empty strings. A focus
      id missing from the new tree is offered to the root as
      focus_petitus, and cleared if it is still missing after
      that.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why do focus_captus, mus_intravit and other derived events arrive after the tree is recomposed?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_addressare dispensator_tractare" via="lib/dispensator.worklog.md">They
      are queued and delivered at the boundary (limen): after the
      current event's actions and the staleness recompose, against the
      NEW tree, so a derived event sees the world its cause produced.
      The queue is copied before delivery, so addressing from inside a
      handler is safe and waits for the next boundary. The cost is a
      second composition for events that produce derived
      ones.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I send an event to one component by its id?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_addressare componens_invenire_in_spatio">dispensator_addressare(d,
      id, genus, tempus). The innermost focus space is taken at the
      call; the id is looked up in that space at the next boundary,
      against the new tree, and an id that is empty or not found there
      is dropped silently. The id chorda is stored, not
      copied, so pass an interned or otherwise long-lived one. Events
      delivered at the boundary skip the ante/post middleware
      hooks.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I intercept every event before the actions see it?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_medium_ponere DispensatorAnte DispensatorPost">Yes,
      with one pair of hooks: dispensator_medium_ponere(d, ante, post,
      ctx). ante runs before the actions and returning VERUM consumes
      the event (no action runs); post runs after the action that
      consumed it, with that component as actor. NIHIL removes a hook.
      They see the raw event and what derivare makes from it, but not
      the events queued for the boundary (hover, focus captured or lost,
      focus_petitus, dispensator_addressare).</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="cur">
    <quaestio! (>why must I call dispensator_finire when the app exits?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_finire motus_effundere motus_gestum_effundere" via="lib/dispensator.worklog.md">Pan/zoom
      and the app's gesture are flushed into the island at a quiet
      seat: when an event or pulse arrives quies_ms (or the gesture's
      own interval) after the last movement, measured on event time so
      a replay flushes at the same events (pan/zoom also flushes when
      focus moves into another nested space). Whatever is still
      pending when the loop ends is lost unless dispensator_finire
      flushes it without waiting.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quid">
    <quaestio! (>what happens with Tab and Escape that no action consumed?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_tractare dispensator_focus">The
      dispatcher handles them itself. Tab (Shift+Tab backwards) cycles
      focus over every focusable component under the pane's root
      (motus.spatium), nested spaces included, wrapping at the ends.
      Escape while focus is inside a nested space moves focus to the
      first focusable of the nearest enclosing level. Consume the key in
      an action to override either.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how does my app learn the window size?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_tractare insula_ramus_attributum">On
      EVENTUS_MUTARE_MAGNITUDINEM the dispatcher writes
      superficies_latitudo and superficies_altitudo (our pixels) into
      the island root as writer "dispensator", before targeting and
      composition, and the componere function reads them there. The
      app's canon must declare both attributes as numbers owned by
      "dispensator", otherwise the island refuses the
      write.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="quomodo">
    <quaestio! (>how do I record the raw events a dispensator receives, for replay or tests?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_notarium_ponere EventusNotatum eventus_notata_scribere_stml">dispensator_notarium_ponere(d,
      xar) with a Xar of EventusNotatum: every raw event, pulses
      included, is appended (not the events derivare makes from it).
      Mouse down, up and move also get scopus, the id of the component
      they were targeted at (copied into the dispatcher's piscina), and
      scopus_x/y in its local space; other events have no scopus.
      eventus_notata_scribere_stml writes the list; NIHIL stops
      recording.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

  <interrogatio genus="possumne">
    <quaestio! (>can I destroy a dispensator and free its memory?
    <responsum symbolum="dispensator_creare piscina_destruere">No:
      there is no dispensator_destruere. dispensator_creare makes three
      piscinae of its own (arbor_a and arbor_b, LXIV * M bytes each to
      start, and destinatio_scratch, XVI * M) that nothing frees, besides what it
      takes from the piscina you pass. Create one per app and keep it
      for the process lifetime.</responsum>
  </interrogatio>

</interrogationes>
